That warning is from my own `getImageData` eval check (not the game). It's a warning caused by the debug eval, not the game itself. Note the previous eval I ran used `getContext('2d')` on the page canvas and getImageData — that triggered the readback warning. Not a game error. To keep "console 100% clean", I should avoid getImageData checks on the game canvas, or acknowledge this warning is from verification tooling. The game's own code uses `willReadFrequently: true` for its normalization canvas. The main canvas readback was mine via eval.

Let me clear and re-verify with AO_DEBUG only (no getImageData). Check state over time: sample AO_DEBUG every ~300ms for combat events. Let me write a small sampling script via playwright-cli eval? playwright-cli eval runs one expression; I can return a promise? Probably not — it awaits? Let me try an eval that samples with setTimeout — no timers available in eval either. Instead, run multiple evals over time via shell loop... but each playwright-cli eval is a separate CLI invocation; the page persists (daemon). Sampling via repeated CLI calls works but each call re-prints. I can write a small node script that connects? Overkill.

Alternative: use a single eval that installs a collector on the page (window.__collector = {events: []}) that polls AO_DEBUG every 250ms via setInterval and records support events. setInterval inside page context — the eval runs in page, so timers work in-page. Then later eval returns the collected events. That's a clean approach.

Collector logic:
```js
window.__ev = [];
if (!window.__collectorInstalled) {
  window.__collectorInstalled = true;
  window.__last = {};
  setInterval(() => {
    const d = window.AO_DEBUG && window.AO_DEBUG();
    if (!d) return;
    for (const f of d.fighters) {
      if (f.immobilized > 0 && !window.__last[f.id]?.par) window.__ev.push({ t: d.elapsed, type: 'paralysis', id: f.id, dur: f.immobilized });
      if (f.support) window.__ev.push({ t: d.elapsed, type: 'support-intent', id: f.id, kind: f.support.kind, ally: f.support.ally, timer: f.support.timer });
      if (f.immobilized === 0 && window.__last[f.id]?.par && d.elapsed - (window.__last[f.id]?.parT ?? 0) > .5) window.__ev.push({ t: d.elapsed, type: 'freed', id: f.id });
      ...
    }
    window.__last[f.id] = { par: f.immobilized > 0, parT: ... };
  }, 200);
}
```
Keep simpler: record transitions: paralysis start, paralysis end (freed), support intents, hp changes of player? Enough: paralysis start/end + intents + cleanse casts. 'Removida' cast → ally.immobilized goes 0 while enemy intended more. The 'freed' event with time since paralysis start tells if removal happened before natural expiry (1.8s).

Also record deaths: alive false.

Plan:
